    "product_id": "80c143b9-be7d-4ee6-96f4-2ea96da422fd",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 181715\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1015 AM MST Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather conditions are expected through this \nweekend with near zero rain chances. \n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are expected through \nat least this weekend with afternoon temperatures near 100 degrees \nacross the lower deserts. \n\n- A low pressure system and moisture return early next week will \nbring a return of rain chances to at least the high terrain of \nnorthern and eastern Arizona. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nDrying conditions with near to slightly above normal temperatures \nare expected today through this weekend. It is still muggy at the\nsurface in south-central AZ, including Phoenix, with dew point \ntemperatures in the 60s to near 70 degrees early this morning, but\ndry air has moved in aloft, which is partly why there were no \naddition showers and storms Thursday after the very wet Wednesday.\nPWATs at midnight yesterday were around 1.7-1.9\", but have since \ndropped to 1.0-1.2\" and will continue falling today through \nSaturday. Surface moisture will also steadily scour out today \nthrough Saturday, falling into the 50s by this evening and into \nthe 40s by Saturday evening in south-central AZ. It will be even \ndrier west of Phoenix. The abundant dry air that will be moving\nthrough can be seen upstream on water vapor satellite imagery. \n\nSatellite also shows a shortwave moving through southeast AZ, which \nmay provide just enough forcing to spark isolated showers and storms \nin the White Mtns this morning and early this afternoon, possibly \nclipping portions of southeastern Gila County. There is also a broad \nupper level low situated over the Northwest CONUS. This low will \ngradually track eastward through this weekend and help suppress H5 \nheights over the Desert Southwest and thus moderating the \ntemperatures. Temperatures will be nearly the same each day, with \nmorning lows in the 70s and afternoon highs around 97-103 degrees \nacross the lower deserts. Normal highs are right around 100 degrees \nfor mid-September. These temperatures will be a nice reprieve, \nespecially the mornings, but will still result in Minor HeatRisk. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nHeading into early next week, there is good confidence that a \nPacific low will move to the central/southern coast of California \nby the end of the day Monday. Exactly how this low evolves once\nreaching the coast is still a little uncertain, but latest model \nconsensus suggests the low will track ENE inland staying mostly \nnorth of the local area and driving a chance for showers and storms \nacross northern AZ. Conceptually this is not a bad setup for a \ntransitional severe weather event with potential frontogenetical \nforcing and a moderately strong upper level jet with higher shear, \nbut rebounding quickly from the very dry air this weekend and \ngetting sufficient moisture in here to support any lower desert rain \nchance will be tough. Model guidance does at least support a slow \nreturn of moisture Monday through next week, more so coming from the \nsoutheast like typical early stages of the monsoon. \n\nModel guidance is also pointing toward a strong ridge developing \nthrough the middle of the U.S. with the subtropical high \nrestrengthening near/over Texas by mid to late week. This would keep \nthe Desert Southwest under predominant southwest flow which is not \nthe most ideal setup for lower desert storm chances, especially not \nfor southeast CA and southwest AZ. Latest NBM forecast has PoPs \nfocused primarily across northern and eastern AZ each day next week \nwith only minor (10-20%) PoPs for south-central AZ lower deserts in \nthe evenings. The one thing this pattern does open the door for, the \nlonger it persists, is potential Eastern Pacific tropical moisture \nadvecting north.\n\nTemperatures through next week will mostly stay the same day-to-day, \nwith highs in the upper 90s to lower 100s, which will be around 2-5 \ndegrees above normal, and lows remaining mostly in the 70s. The \ntemperatures will lead to widespread Minor HeatRisk through at least \nthe first half of the week with a trend toward areas of Moderate \nHeatRisk by the second half of the week as morning low temperatures \ncreep back up.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1715Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major weather issues will exist through Saturday afternoon under \na few mountain cumulus build-ups and patchy high cirrus. Confidence \nis good that east winds will only gradually switch to westerly \nmid/late afternoon with a prolonged period centered around a \nsoutherly cross runway trajectory (i.e. 140v230) with speeds nearing \n10kt. A reversal back to easterly is not likely until after \nmidnight. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather issues will exist through Saturday afternoon under clear \nskies. Winds will favor a W/NW direction at KIPL and S/SW \ntrajectory at KBLH. Speeds should remain under 12kt with limited \ngusts, and extended periods nearly calm conditions or light \nnortherly in the morning. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nOverall there are no fire weather concerns through the next week. A \ndrying trend will continue today through this weekend. With no rain \nexpected through at least the start of next week. Minimum RH values \nthis afternoon will be around 20-35% across south-central AZ to 10-\n20% across southeast CA and southwest AZ. The minimum RHs will then \ndip to 10-20% for nearly all areas this weekend. Overnight \nrecoveries across south-central AZ will still be good tonight at 45-\n75%, but then falls lower this weekend. There will be a gradual \nreturn of moisture next week, best across eastern AZ and there will \nbe an increase in rain chances again, but as of now rain chances \nlook to primarily favor northern and eastern AZ high terrain. \nBesides a potential increase in breeziness early next week with a \nweather system, winds will mostly follow typical diurnal patterns \nwith modest afternoon upslope/upvalley gustiness (15-25 mph).\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict\n",
